"""Original whole-home coordination manuscript; independent case arithmetic."""
CHAPTERS=[]
def chapter(title,deck,paragraphs,example,rows,conclusion,prompts,checks,task):
    CHAPTERS.append(dict(title=title,deck=deck,paragraphs=paragraphs,example=example,rows=rows,conclusion=conclusion,prompts=prompts,checks=checks,task=task))

chapter('Define the whole-home outcome','Coordinate rooms around one household decision.',[
'Write what the completed home must allow the household to do. Describe room use, access, comfort and continuity needs before listing products. A whole-home plan should explain why the work belongs together and which outcomes remain essential if the project must be reduced.',
'Separate required outcomes, preferred improvements and optional choices. Give each a reason and an acceptance record. An owner can define a functional goal while the qualified design team determines the technical solution. Do not turn an attractive image into an unreviewed construction instruction.',
'Record constraints including funding, occupied periods, access, other commitments and unresolved assessments. Identify the people who make selections and approve spending. A shared outcome statement prevents individual room choices from quietly exceeding the household purpose.',
'Use the brief as the baseline for later package decisions. If the purpose changes, revise the brief and dependent scope before claiming the budget is still for the original project. Retain the change rationale rather than rewriting history to make every result appear planned.'
], 'A fictional household lists twelve desired outcomes: five essential, four preferred and three optional. No category is a technical approval or a cost estimate.', [('Essential','5 outcomes'),('Preferred','4 outcomes'),('Optional','3 outcomes'),('Total brief','12 outcomes')],
'Priority counts help organize a discussion. The individual outcome, assessed scope and actual cost still need review.',
['Household outcomes / room uses / desired function','Essential / preferred / optional priorities and reasons','Funding / timing / occupancy / access constraints','Design and approval responsibilities / decision owners','Brief version / acceptance evidence / revision triggers'],
['Outcomes are separate from technical solutions.','Spending authority is explicit.','Changed goals trigger scope review.'], 'Agree on the household brief before requesting room-by-room product selections.')

chapter('Create the property and room baseline','Every later total needs a shared map.',[
'Collect existing plans, reports, product records and accessible photographs. Assign an ID to each room or project zone and identify retained areas. Label unverified drawings and approximate dimensions so a contractor cannot mistake them for confirmed design information.',
'Record current use, known prior work and observed condition by zone. Keep technical assessments in separate provider reports. Do not open equipment, disturb materials or enter hazardous spaces to complete the owner record.',
'Use the same zone IDs for scope, selections, quotations, changes and handover. Shared hallways, external services and project-wide work need their own identifiers so they are not counted in several room budgets.',
'Maintain a current document register with version, date and responsible author. Archive superseded plans without letting them circulate as current instructions. A clear baseline reduces contradictions between otherwise well-prepared room packages.'
], 'A fictional home plan contains eight room zones and two shared project zones. The shared access and service zones are not duplicated inside each room package.', [('Room zones','8'),('Shared project zones','2'),('Total indexed zones','10'),('Shared zones counted per room','No')],
'The index does not verify dimensions or site condition. It supplies a common reference for the responsible providers.',
['Room and shared-zone IDs / retained areas','Plans / dates / authors / verified status','Accessible condition records / prior reports','Common references for scope and cost lines','Current document register / superseded versions'],
['Approximate records remain labeled.','Shared scope has its own ID.','Only current versions inform decisions.'], 'Create the zone index and document register before combining contractor estimates.')

chapter('Resolve assessment and design gates','A budget cannot approve an unknown technical condition.',[
'List the assessments and design decisions needed before each package can be priced reliably. Assign the qualified provider, required information and decision date. A structural, moisture, service-capacity or material concern should not disappear inside a general contingency line.',
'Distinguish an owner observation, a professional finding and an approved design record. Keep their authors and dates visible. If a finding requires more investigation, retain the next action instead of assuming that a preliminary report resolves every condition.',
'For relevant older-property work, EPA lead renovation guidance identifies important pre-disturbance questions [2]. Ask appropriately qualified providers to determine applicable requirements and scope. This planner supplies no testing, removal, design or technical clearance procedure.',
'Connect each unresolved gate to affected quantities, products, dates and costs. Mark the estimate conditional where necessary. Do not authorize downstream procurement solely because a draft budget contains a number for the affected work.'
], 'A fictional gate register has four decisions: two resolved with reports, one awaiting design and one awaiting an assessment. Three work packages depend on the two open decisions.', [('Resolved gates','2'),('Open gates','2'),('Affected work packages','3'),('Final downstream order approval','Pending')],
'Two completed reports do not make all dependent packages ready. Track each dependency to its actual resolution.',
['Gate question / zone / affected package','Qualified provider / required report / due date','Observation / finding / design status and evidence','Open condition / quantity / selection / cost effect','Downstream approval limits / closure reference'],
['Technical gates have a responsible provider.','Unresolved findings remain conditional.','Procurement waits for relevant decisions.'], 'Ask the design and assessment team to confirm the gates needed for each major package.')

chapter('Build work packages and assign interfaces','A complete home is more than the sum of room quotes.',[
'Group the work into packages that can be described, priced and checked. Use clear boundaries for removal, preparation, services, finishes, protection and restoration as relevant. Keep cross-room systems and project-wide logistics separate where that makes responsibility clearer.',
'For each interface, state who designs, supplies, installs, connects, checks and restores the adjacent work. A kitchen installer and an electrical provider may each assume that another party handles a related task. An interface matrix should expose the gap before work begins.',
'Give every required task one accountable owner. Shared responsibility can still be coordinated, but the record needs a named point of accountability and evidence of completion. Do not assume that the owner can perform an unassigned technical task.',
'Review interfaces when a package changes contractor or sequence. A cost saving inside one package may create an additional visit or restoration cost elsewhere. Keep the overall outcome and funding effect visible before approving the change.'
], 'A fictional five-package project has four interfaces. Three have confirmed owners; one utility connection is awaiting provider confirmation.', [('Work packages','5'),('Interfaces','4'),('Confirmed interface owners','3'),('Open interface','1')],
'The open interface is a scope gap, even when all five package totals are available.',
['Package IDs / included and retained boundaries','Design / supply / install / connect / restore roles','Cross-package interfaces / accountable owners','Completion evidence / approval and access needs','Changed providers / dependent scope / revised costs'],
['Every required interface has a status.','Technical tasks are not assigned by assumption.','Package savings are checked across the whole plan.'], 'Review one interface matrix with all relevant providers before selecting separate package offers.')

chapter('Choose priorities and a practical phase plan','Deferral can move cost rather than remove it.',[
'Rank packages against the agreed outcomes and technical dependencies. Identify work that must precede another package, work that can be deferred without losing completed work, and optional improvements that can be omitted. Have the responsible professionals confirm technical implications of phasing.',
'Compare doing related work together with splitting it into phases. Repeated mobilization, protection, access, temporary arrangements or restoration can change the total. Do not claim a phase plan saves money solely because the first-stage payment is lower.',
'Keep each phase functionally defined, with completion conditions and household access needs. An unfinished interface may leave later costs or temporary obligations. Record them rather than describing a partly complete phase as the finished desired outcome.',
'Review cash, disruption and total cost together. A household may reasonably prefer a more expensive phased approach because of constraints, but the tradeoff should be explicit. Keep the chosen sequence with its assumed future work and funding.'
], 'An invented combined package costs $30,000. Two matched phases cost $18,000 and $14,000 because repeated setup is expressly included in the latter.', [('Combined work','$30,000'),('Phase 1','$18,000'),('Phase 2','$14,000'),('Phased total','$32,000'),('Phasing premium','$2,000')],
'The smaller first phase does not reduce the matched total. Future prices and actual technical feasibility remain unverified.',
['Outcome priorities / dependency evidence','Combined versus phased scope and assumptions','Repeated setup / protection / temporary costs','Phase completion / household function / interfaces','Cash needs / total-cost tradeoff / selected sequence'],
['Technical phasing is professionally reviewed.','Deferral is not labeled automatic saving.','Each phase has a defined usable outcome.'], 'Price repeated services before choosing phases only on the basis of the first payment.')

chapter('Set the whole-home funding boundary','Separate the construction comparison from all household costs.',[
'Define a working ceiling for the whole project and identify the confirmed funding sources without counting uncertain proceeds as available cash. Keep borrowing terms and personal financial choices outside a simple contractor comparison. Obtain appropriate advice for commitments that need it.',
'Include design, assessments, approvals, owner purchases, temporary arrangements and reserve where relevant. A contractor sum is not automatically the amount the household needs. Label known quotes, provisional allowances and unresolved costs separately.',
'Use one funding model for all packages and shared services. If the same temporary accommodation cost appears in several room budgets, reconcile it to one project line. Keep optional work separate so a reduction decision does not conceal an essential obligation.',
'Compare the complete forecast against the ceiling and show the gap. A positive variance needs a concrete scope, funding or timing decision. Reducing the reserve without reviewing uncertainty does not make the underlying project cheaper.'
], 'A fictional plan has $80,000 package work, $8,000 professional services, $3,000 temporary costs and $9,000 reserve against a $95,000 ceiling.', [('Defined packages','$80,000'),('Professional services','$8,000'),('Temporary costs','$3,000'),('Reserve','$9,000'),('Funding plan / ceiling','$100,000 / $95,000'),('Over-ceiling gap','$5,000')],
'The reserve is available funding for uncertainty, not approved scope or a price quote. Resolve the $5,000 gap before commitment.',
['Working ceiling / confirmed sources / timing','Packages / professional / approval / owner costs','Temporary arrangements / shared-scope reconciliation','Reserve rationale / optional work / open costs','Forecast less ceiling / decisions / updated plan'],
['Contractor totals are not the entire funding plan.','Shared owner costs appear once.','The gap has a named decision owner.'], 'Build one all-in funding boundary before authorizing separate packages.')

chapter('Create a quantity register across packages','The same area can support several different legitimate quantities.',[
'Record each quantity with its zone, surface or system, unit, source and responsible verifier. A room floor area can inform flooring, while wall area informs paint. Do not copy one square-foot value into unrelated work simply because the units look familiar.',
'Keep installed net quantities distinct from purchased material quantities and fixed charges. A supplier pack allowance does not automatically increase labor area. Ask the providers to confirm the basis of each rate before using the quantity register to compare bids.',
'Identify repeated and shared work explicitly. The same protection or demolition scope may appear in several package offers even when the project needs it once. Reconcile through written scope confirmation rather than deleting lines purely because their names resemble each other.',
'Retain measurement revisions and trace their effect on dependent orders and costs. A changed wall location can alter floor area, wall coating, services and trim. The register supports arithmetic checks without verifying design or authorizing construction.'
], 'A fictional rectangular 12 × 10 ft room with 8 ft walls has 120 sq ft of floor and 352 sq ft of gross wall area before openings. The ceiling is separately 120 sq ft.', [('Floor: 12 × 10','120 sq ft'),('Walls: 2 × (12 + 10) × 8','352 sq ft'),('Level ceiling','120 sq ft'),('One common quantity for all trades','Incorrect')],
'Equal units do not imply equal surfaces or scope. Each provider must confirm the actual takeoff and pricing basis.',
['Quantity ID / zone / surface or system / unit','Measurement source / date / professional verifier','Net installed / purchased quantity / rate basis','Shared work / overlap / provider confirmation','Revisions / affected packages / updated orders'],
['Surface identities remain visible.','Material and labor bases are distinguished.','Measurement changes reach dependent records.'], 'Link each major cost line to a verified quantity or an explicitly defined fixed scope.')

chapter('Keep selections and procurement synchronized','A product choice is also an interface and timing decision.',[
'Create a selection register with exact product references, affected zones, approval status and required decision dates. Record who confirms technical suitability and who places the order. A visual approval by the owner is separate from a professional compatibility decision.',
'Tie each purchase to the current design, verified dimensions and required supporting work. Check lead time, delivery, storage, return terms and any conditioning requirements with the responsible providers. An early delivery is not proof that the site is ready for installation.',
'Price substitutions against a complete baseline. A cheaper fixture may require different connections or restoration, while a delayed product may affect several packages. Ask for the overall installed and schedule effect before approving a replacement.',
'Reconcile allowances with actual selections as they become firm. Remove the old allowance and replace it using the agreed terms. Keep actual order status distinct from a quotation, tentative availability or an owner wish list.'
], 'A fictional substitution saves $200 on a product but adds $120 interface work and $100 delivery. All three changes are confirmed against the same baseline.', [('Product difference','−$200'),('Interface addition','$120'),('Delivery addition','$100'),('Net project increase','$20')],
'The product is cheaper while the defined project becomes $20 more expensive. Other scope or timing effects must still be checked.',
['Exact selection / zone / owner and technical approvals','Design and dimension references / order authority','Lead time / delivery / storage / return terms','Substitution baseline / dependent work / timing','Allowance replacement / actual order / confirmation'],
['Owner appearance approval is not technical approval.','Substitutions are priced across interfaces.','Quotes and placed orders have distinct statuses.'], 'Check the full installed effect before approving any product substitution.')

chapter('Map services and other-trade dependencies','Cross-room systems need one coordination record.',[
'Ask the qualified design and trade providers to identify the service implications of the project. Changes to equipment, rooms or layouts may affect electrical, plumbing, heating, cooling or other systems. This planner performs no load, sizing, routing or capacity calculation.',
'Record assessed requirements, responsible providers and the design version each package uses. Keep retained components and proposed changes explicit. A room package should not assume that an existing shared system is suitable without the relevant review.',
'Assign interfaces for utility coordination, access, required approvals, professional checks and restoration. Ask who confirms each prerequisite before a downstream trade starts. A complete room finish price can still omit the work needed to support it.',
'Review alternatives at the whole-home level. A different system choice may change several package costs and the occupied-house sequence. Retain the professional explanation and compare complete scenarios rather than making a design decision from one equipment price.'
], 'A fictional upgrade has $4,000 equipment supply, $1,500 assessed connection work and $500 agreed restoration, with no overlap between providers.', [('Equipment','$4,000'),('Supporting connections','$1,500'),('Restoration','$500'),('Defined system scenario','$6,000')],
'The $4,000 equipment line is not the installed system total. Actual suitability and required work are professionally determined.',
['Proposed system changes / retained elements','Qualified assessment / capacity questions / design versions','Provider and utility interfaces / prerequisites','Connection / approval / checking / restoration scope','Complete alternatives / cost / household sequence'],
['No owner sizing or capacity shortcut is used.','Shared systems have a responsible reviewer.','Supporting work is included in the scenario.'], 'Ask the relevant providers to review the same whole-home service-change map.')

chapter('Track approvals and required completion records','A fee allowance is not an approval.',[
'Ask the appropriate local authority and responsible professionals which approvals and documentation apply to the actual project. Record the question, responsible party, submitted version and status. Do not infer requirements from a generic online checklist or another jurisdiction.',
'Separate an estimated fee, a submitted application, an issued approval and a completed required inspection. Each is a different stage. Record conditions and dependencies so later work is not scheduled on the assumption that every stage is already complete.',
'Assign responsibility for preparing documents, making submissions, arranging visits and retaining final records. A contractor package may include some services and exclude others. Resolve the boundary before treating a permit allowance as a complete approval process.',
'Review changed work with the responsible parties before relying on an earlier approval. This planner provides no code interpretation, legal determination or authorization to build. It organizes the evidence trail needed to ask accurate questions.'
], 'A fictional approval register has six items: two issued, two submitted, one awaiting documents and one awaiting confirmation of applicability.', [('Issued','2'),('Submitted','2'),('Awaiting documents','1'),('Applicability unresolved','1'),('All items ready','No')],
'Six listed items do not equal six approvals. Track each actual status and its effect on the sequence.',
['Relevant authority / professional / applicability question','Approval IDs / current documents / responsible party','Fee assumption / submission / issued status','Conditions / required visits / completion evidence','Changed work / review trigger / retained final record'],
['Estimated fees are distinct from approvals.','Submission is not treated as issuance.','Local requirements have a verified source.'], 'Confirm the approval and completion-record responsibilities with the actual authority and project team.')

chapter('Plan occupancy, protection and temporary living','Household continuity has a scope and a cost.',[
'Map the rooms and routes available during each phase, with access and use guidance from the responsible providers. Record temporary kitchen, bathroom, storage or accommodation arrangements where relevant. Do not assume the household can occupy every area simply because work is limited to one package.',
'Assign protection, furniture moves, cleanup, security and other agreed services. Identify tasks needing qualified providers. An owner task should be explicit and practical, not a hidden assumption that the household will perform technical or hazardous work.',
'Price shared temporary arrangements for the actual period and avoid repeating them in every room budget. Separate known bookings from provisional scenarios. Record what changes if work runs longer, including the terms and approval process for extra charges.',
'Keep communication and contingency contacts with the phase plan. If a prerequisite fails, ask the responsible provider to revise access and use guidance. The owner record is a coordination tool and does not provide a site-safety or occupancy approval.'
], 'A fictional temporary-living scenario uses 20 nights at $90, one $250 move and $150 storage. All are separate owner costs.', [('20 × $90','$1,800'),('One move','$250'),('Storage','$150'),('Temporary-living scenario','$2,200')],
'The nights and rate are invented. Confirm actual arrangements and whether costs are already included elsewhere.',
['Phase / usable rooms / routes / provider guidance','Temporary facilities / accommodation / booking status','Protection / moves / cleanup / responsible owners','Period / rate / fixed charges / overlap check','Delay terms / revised use plan / contacts'],
['Use guidance comes from responsible providers.','Shared living costs appear once.','Extended time has an explicit cost scenario.'], 'Agree on household arrangements before accepting a sequence that removes essential rooms from use.')

chapter('Build the package cost plan','A consolidated total needs one treatment of shared scope.',[
'List current accepted or proposed package costs with their status and source. Keep known fixed scope, allowances, options and unresolved work separate. A collection of numbers from different design versions is not a coherent whole-home estimate.',
'Reconcile shared services such as access, protection, disposal and mobilization. Confirm with providers where overlap actually exists before changing the totals. A similar line name does not prove duplication, and deleting necessary work can create an understated plan.',
'Add owner and professional costs on their own lines, then show reserve separately. Keep tax and fee inclusions explicit. The consolidated plan should explain what is still excluded and what evidence would make the forecast more reliable.',
'Compare package revisions against the same baseline and record why they changed. Use complete alternatives for scope decisions. A cheaper room option can create an additional cost in another package that must enter the same project forecast.'
], 'A fictional project has package totals of $25,000, $30,000 and $20,000. Providers confirm that a $1,000 shared protection service is duplicated and one occurrence is credited.', [('Package sum','$75,000'),('Confirmed duplicate credit','−$1,000'),('Reconciled packages','$74,000'),('Owner costs and reserve','Separate funding lines')],
'Only the confirmed overlap is removed. The $74,000 is not the complete funding plan until other obligations are added.',
['Packages / prices / current versions / status','Fixed / allowance / option / unresolved categories','Confirmed overlaps / credits / required shared work','Owner / professional / tax / fee inclusions','Reconciled total / excluded work / forecast confidence'],
['Packages use compatible current assumptions.','Overlap removal has provider evidence.','Reserve remains separate from defined work.'], 'Reconcile shared scope with the providers before adding package prices into the project total.')

chapter('Record uncertainty without spending the reserve twice','A risk allowance is not an approved change.',[
'List unresolved conditions with affected packages, available evidence and the person responsible for assessment. Describe what would trigger additional work and how the scope would be confirmed. A generic percentage reserve cannot explain every uncertainty.',
'Use clearly labeled scenarios to examine funding exposure, without pretending to know the probability or exact cost of an unknown condition. Keep overlapping scenarios visible so the same cause is not funded several times without explanation.',
'Separate reserve held from approved changes funded by it. When work is approved, move its cost into the forecast and reduce remaining reserve under the chosen tracking method. Do not add the approved charge while leaving the original reserve assumption presented as fully uncommitted.',
'Review remaining uncertainty after each major gate and phase. A resolved assessment may change scope, schedule or reserve needs. Record the decision and continue to distinguish funding available from money actually committed.'
], 'A fictional funding plan holds $9,000 reserve. A $1,500 change is assessed and approved from it, with no new funding added.', [('Opening reserve','$9,000'),('Approved transfer into work','−$1,500'),('Remaining reserve','$7,500'),('Increase to total available funds','$0')],
'The approved change becomes defined forecast work. Moving reserve into work does not create new money.',
['Uncertainty / affected packages / assessment owner','Trigger / evidence / scenario / overlapping causes','Opening reserve / approved change / tracking method','Remaining uncommitted reserve / new risks','Review dates / resolved gates / funding decisions'],
['Scenarios are not guaranteed costs.','Approved changes leave the uncommitted reserve.','One cause is not silently counted several times.'], 'Reconcile the reserve after every approved change and assessment milestone.')

chapter('Choose a procurement and contractor structure','Separate packages can introduce coordination obligations.',[
'Compare a coordinated main-contract approach with owner-managed separate packages using the actual scope and capabilities of the project team. Identify who owns sequencing, interfaces, technical coordination and completion. A lower package sum may exclude services included in another offer.',
'Use common request documents, scope categories and evidence checks. FTC contractor guidance supports checking providers and reviewing written estimates and agreements [1]. Keep relevant verification current and use appropriate local advice for the actual commitments.',
'Price coordination and repeated services explicitly where they differ. Identify who handles a missed prerequisite, product conflict or delayed handoff. Do not treat the owner time and capacity required for coordination as an unlimited free resource.',
'Record the rationale for the selected structure and its limits. The planner does not determine the legal role or liability of any participant. Align accepted responsibilities with the actual contract documents and qualified advice.'
], 'A fictional coordinated offer is $78,000. Separate matched packages total $74,000, with $2,000 separately quoted coordination and $1,000 repeated setup.', [('Coordinated offer','$78,000'),('Separate packages','$74,000'),('Coordination and repeated setup','$3,000'),('Comparable separate scenario','$77,000'),('Scenario difference','$1,000')],
'The $4,000 headline gap narrows to $1,000 under the stated assumptions. Suitability and contractual responsibilities still require review.',
['Procurement alternatives / common scope / exclusions','Coordination and interface responsibilities','Provider evidence / local review / accepted documents','Repeated services / owner capacity / complete costs','Selected structure / rationale / unresolved conditions'],
['Coordination scope is compared explicitly.','Owner capacity is not assumed unlimited.','The structure has documented responsibilities.'], 'Compare complete coordination scenarios before selecting a contractor structure on price alone.')

chapter('Link the master sequence to payment timing','The project can run out of cash before exceeding its total budget.',[
'Create a sequence of packages and their prerequisites using the provider-confirmed work plan. Record owner decisions, product lead times, approvals and handoffs. Keep estimated dates distinct from confirmed arrangements and label calendar versus working days.',
'Map accepted payment obligations onto the sequence. Reconcile deposits and milestones to each package total so amounts are not added twice. Actual payment rights, limits and conditions require review of the written terms and applicable local requirements.',
'Track opening available funds, planned inflows and due payments by period. A positive end-of-project balance does not eliminate an early funding gap. Keep unpaid commitments beside current cash to show what is already spoken for.',
'When timing changes, update dependent tasks and cash dates together. A delayed delivery can move labor and temporary-living obligations. Record the complete timing and cost effect before approving a revised sequence.'
], 'A fictional period opens with $20,000, receives a confirmed $10,000 inflow and has $34,000 of accepted payments due.', [('Opening available cash','$20,000'),('Confirmed inflow','$10,000'),('Payments due','−$34,000'),('Period funding gap','−$4,000')],
'The gap needs a real funding or agreed scheduling decision. The arithmetic does not authorize late payment or recommend borrowing.',
['Master prerequisites / dates / provider confirmations','Package milestones / amounts / deposit reconciliation','Opening cash / confirmed inflows / due dates','Unpaid commitments / period funding gaps','Timing changes / owner costs / agreed funding decisions'],
['Payments match accepted terms and totals.','Early cash gaps remain visible.','Schedule revisions reach the cash plan.'], 'Check the first major payment periods before committing the full package sequence.')

chapter('Approve changes across the whole home','A local change can travel through several packages.',[
'Record the requested change, its reason and the current baseline before asking for a price. Identify affected rooms, products, drawings and interfaces. An owner request should not become an assumed instruction to proceed before the defined approval process is complete.',
'Ask the relevant providers for the complete incremental material, labor, supporting-work and timing effect. Include credits for replaced scope where confirmed. A room-level upgrade price may omit dependent service or restoration work.',
'Show the revised forecast and reserve effect before approval. Keep pending options separate from accepted obligations. If an urgent situation needs a different process, obtain appropriate qualified guidance and preserve the actual decision evidence.',
'Update selections, orders, dates and handover requirements after approval. A signed change price alone does not keep every dependent record current. Retain the earlier version so the final record explains how the project evolved.'
], 'A fictional layout change adds $800 room work, $350 assessed service relocation and $200 restoration, with a confirmed $150 credit for omitted work.', [('Room work','$800'),('Service relocation','$350'),('Restoration','$200'),('Omitted-work credit','−$150'),('Net approved addition','$1,200')],
'The $800 room line understates the complete change. The $1,200 still depends on the stated scope and confirmed credit.',
['Change request / reason / baseline / affected zones','Provider-priced interfaces / credits / schedule effects','Pending or approved / decision authority / evidence','Forecast / reserve / funding gap before approval','Updated drawings / selections / orders / completion needs'],
['Downstream work is included in the change.','Confirmed credits are applied once.','Approval reaches dependent project records.'], 'Review the complete cross-package effect before authorizing a room-level change.')

chapter('Review progress using obligations and evidence','Money paid is not the same as work complete.',[
'For each package, keep accepted value, approved changes, paid amounts, unpaid obligations and remaining uncommitted scope distinct. A high payment percentage does not demonstrate the same percentage of technical completion. Use the actual required provider and authority evidence.',
'Forecast remaining cost from current obligations and assessed work, not by subtracting payments from an outdated ceiling. Include confirmed credits and unresolved items with their status. Preserve the original baseline for variance review.',
'Review the whole-home forecast at useful package gates. Explain changes by scope, quantity, rate, timing or new findings. An unexplained revised total is less useful than a smaller set of traceable differences.',
'Assign corrective decisions and dates to open gaps. The owner record supports coordination but does not certify workmanship or authorize payment contrary to the agreement. Retain the evidence behind both cost and completion decisions.'
], 'A fictional package has $20,000 accepted scope, $1,000 approved additions and $500 confirmed credits. Payments are $12,000, with no other scope changes.', [('Defined forecast: 20,000 + 1,000 − 500','$20,500'),('Paid','$12,000'),('Remaining payment obligation','$8,500'),('Technical completion percentage','Not inferred from payment')],
'The $8,500 is unpaid defined cost, not necessarily unperformed work. Review actual completion evidence separately.',
['Accepted scope / changes / confirmed credits','Paid / unpaid / uncommitted / unresolved categories','Completion evidence / responsible provider / status','Current forecast / baseline / variance explanation','Decision owners / dates / revised package actions'],
['Payments do not stand in for completion.','Forecast uses current obligations.','Every material variance has a reason.'], 'Review cost and completion side by side at each major package handover.')

chapter('Close out by room, system and interface','One finished room cannot close every project obligation.',[
'Create a handover matrix covering room packages, shared systems and interfaces. List the completion evidence, outstanding items and responsible provider for each. Distinguish an owner walk-through from required technical checks or authority records.',
'Record the punch list with locations, clear observations and agreed follow-up. Keep technical diagnosis with the appropriate provider. Save actual product references, warranties, care instructions and useful approved documents in one indexed owner file.',
'Reconcile final quantities, retained materials, returns, approved changes and confirmed credits. Keep requested credits and disputed items separate from confirmed amounts. The final payment decision must use the actual terms and relevant advice rather than an invented workbook rule.',
'Close each item only when its evidence is available and update the next-action owner for anything still open. A project may be largely usable while some obligations remain. Retain those obligations instead of describing the entire project as complete without qualification.'
], 'A fictional handover has ten packages: seven accepted with records, two with punch-list items and one awaiting a required document.', [('Accepted with records','7'),('Punch-list packages','2'),('Awaiting document','1'),('Open packages','3')],
'Seven closed packages do not close the remaining three. The count is not a technical completion percentage.',
['Room / system / interface handover matrix','Required evidence / provider / authority / status','Punch list / observations / dates / responsible owner','Product labels / warranties / care / document index','Final obligations / credits / payment and closure records'],
['Technical records are distinct from a visual review.','Open obligations survive partial handover.','Credits and final costs have evidence.'], 'Reconcile every package and interface before archiving the whole-home project.')

chapter('Convert the project file into a care plan','Keep the information needed for the next owner decision.',[
'Transfer actual installed asset details, product documents and provider contacts into a maintenance register. Record warranty and care instructions for the selected products. The project selection list should be updated where substitutions changed what was installed.',
'Create next-action tasks from actual instructions and qualified recommendations. Do not invent universal annual intervals or assume new equipment needs no attention. Keep unresolved punch-list work distinct from ordinary future maintenance.',
'Review the financial result against the original outcomes and scope. Separate final project spending from unused reserve and future maintenance contributions. Lessons should explain which assumptions changed, not merely whether the project ended under a chosen number.',
'Retain current plans, approved changes, invoices and completion records in an accessible indexed file. Record who can update it and where copies are held. A clear owner record improves later decisions without guaranteeing resale value or future reliability.'
], 'A fictional project had $100,000 funding available and ends with $92,000 of confirmed final cost. The remaining $8,000 is unspent funding, with no outstanding project obligations assumed.', [('Available project funds','$100,000'),('Final confirmed cost','−$92,000'),('Unspent funding','$8,000'),('Future maintenance cost','Separate plan')],
'Unspent funds are not proof that every original assumption was correct. Review outcome, scope and open obligations before reallocating money.',
['Actual installed assets / substitutions / provider contacts','Current manuals / warranties / care requirements','Next tasks / source / responsible owner / open defects','Final spending / unused funds / original scope comparison','Indexed archive / update owner / lessons for later decisions'],
['The register describes actual installed products.','Care tasks use actual sources.','Future funding is separate from final project cost.'], 'Finish with an indexed owner file and a sourced next-action plan for the completed home.')

SOURCES=[('1','FTC: Home Repair Scams','https://consumer.ftc.gov/features/pass-it-on/home-repair-scams'),('2','EPA: Lead Renovation, Repair and Painting','https://www.epa.gov/lead/lead-renovation-repair-and-painting-program')]
